  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "In the highest-transfer verapamil cirrhosis context, all L1/L2 absolute oral bioavailability measurements are 22-81.5%, with 52.3% in the main cirrhotic cohort, supporting F >= 20% for the verapamil-like query.",
      "card_ids": ["C11", "C13", "C15", "C43", "C48", "C55", "C114", "C127"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 and L5 show verapamil has 100% human intestinal absorption, is BCS high permeability with complete intestinal absorption, and has high Caco-2/jejunal permeability, with P-gp efflux playing a minor role for this highly permeable scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C190", "C216", "C189", "C235", "C260", "C254", "C265", "C237", "C251", "C257", "C277", "C242", "C281", "C263"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Where verapamil's low oral bioavailability is discussed, it is attributed to extensive first-pass metabolism rather than efflux, and intestinal CYP3A4 activity did not alter permeability; this does not provide affirmative evidence for F < 20% in the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C264", "C263", "C281"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Structurally related valbenazine has human absolute F approximately 49%, and no transferable context provides affirmative F < 20% evidence; sub-20% values are animal/species-specific outliers.",
      "card_ids": ["C28", "C79", "C92", "C95", "C107", "C70", "C99", "C109", "C118", "C86", "C132", "C78", "C85"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C274 and C280 show verapamil is a P-gp substrate and P-gp/CYP3A4 inhibition increases its permeability/exposure, confirming some gut-wall efflux/metabolism; however, these mechanism-level observations are outweighed by direct human F values >=20% in transferable contexts.",
      "card_ids": ["C274", "C280", "C11", "C13", "C15", "C43", "C48", "C55", "C114", "C127"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": [
        "C260", "C254", "C265", "C237", "C257", "C277", "C242", "C251",
        "C281", "C263", "C264", "C274", "C280", "C252", "C273", "C278", "C247"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement for the query; prediction relies on structural transfer from reference contexts.",
    "L5 records are for reference molecules, not the query; query-specific gut-wall efflux and intestinal metabolism are unmeasured.",
    "First-pass metabolism (hepatic/intestinal) for the query is unmeasured; the lactam and benzocyclobutene motifs lack direct reference F data.",
    "The highest-transfer verapamil context is in cirrhosis, which may overestimate F relative to normal conditions; however all reported values remain >=20%.",
    "No formulation-, dose-, or species-specific PK data for the query are provided.",
    "The single alfentanil 1% report is unexplained and discordant with other alfentanil data."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal metabolism records for the most transferable verapamil scaffold show high intestinal permeability and essentially complete absorption despite P-gp substrate status, with efflux playing a minor role and low F attributed to first-pass metabolism rather than efflux. This supports the absorption component of F >= 20% and does not provide affirmative sub-20% evidence. Combined with L1/L2 absolute F values of 22-81.5% for verapamil and ~49% for valbenazine, the prediction remains high with moderate confidence."
}
